EP4CE6 Bitstream Reverse Engineering — Dual License
=====================================================

As of 2026-04-07, this project uses a dual-license scheme, replacing the
previous MIT license. The choice is deliberate: the purpose of this
work is to keep FPGA toolchain research in hacker hands, not to hand
Altera/Intel a free patch to close the holes we pried open.

----------------------------------------------------------------------
(1) CODE — GNU General Public License v3.0 or later (GPL-3.0-or-later)
----------------------------------------------------------------------

All source code in this repository is licensed under GPL-3.0-or-later.
This includes, without limitation:

  * fuzz/**/*.py       (fuzzing pipeline, codecs, analysis tools)
  * fuzz/**/*.v        (Verilog generators and jailbreak scanners)
  * fuzz/**/*.tcl      (Quartus driver scripts)
  * any future C/C++/Rust implementations of the RouteCodec / LutCodec

The full license text is at LICENSES/GPL-3.0-or-later.txt. Every source
file should carry the SPDX identifier:

    SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-or-later

The practical consequence: if you link, fork, or vendor this code into
another project (including a commercial Quartus replacement, or Altera's
own toolchain), **your project must be released under GPLv3 as well**,
with full source. Static linking, dynamic linking, and the GPL's usual
derived-work rules apply.

----------------------------------------------------------------------
(2) DOCUMENTATION, FINDINGS & METHODOLOGY
    — Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International
----------------------------------------------------------------------

All prose, diagrams, tables, reverse-engineering reports, memory files,
and documentation in this repository are licensed under CC BY-SA 4.0.
This includes, without limitation:

  * README.md, README_zh.md
  * CLAUDE.md
  * results/FINDINGS.md (and any future reports)
  * any blog posts, slide decks, or papers derived from this work

The full license text is at LICENSES/CC-BY-SA-4.0.txt.

The practical consequence: if you cite, excerpt, or reuse any of the
CRAM models, the C4/R4/LI address formulas, the CE6→CE10 jailbreak
findings, the RBF CRC specification, or the XOR-chain dead-cell
scanning methodology in a paper, tutorial, talk, or independent work,
you must (a) credit this repository, and (b) release your derivative
work under CC BY-SA 4.0 (or a later compatible version). Knowledge
about this silicon stays open.

----------------------------------------------------------------------
Scope notes
----------------------------------------------------------------------

* Bitstream data files (*.rbf, *.sof, *.sqlite) are raw hardware
  telemetry, not creative works; no license is asserted over them.
  Redistribution is subject to Altera/Intel's original bitstream terms,
  which are not granted by this repository.

* Quartus-generated intermediate files in work/ and results/rbf/ are
  not covered and should not be redistributed from this repository.

* The AX301 pin map, XOR-chain scanner methodology, and all novel
  CRAM/routing models described in README.md are original research by
  the project authors and fall under the CC BY-SA 4.0 grant above.

